# Placeholders, none of them work. `cp env.example .env && chmod 600 .env`
# scripts/deploy/RUNBOOK.md section 3.4 is the authority on the values.
# Not named .env.example because .gitignore swallows .env.* and it would not
# reach a clone. Read only by `docker compose`, from the project directory.

# The commit SHA to run. Never `latest`; the deploy job rewrites this line.
KEEL_IMAGE_TAG=REPLACE_WITH_A_COMMIT_SHA

# The database this stack runs and owns, as of 11 September 2026. For one day it
# used the host's Postgres and took a KEEL_DSN here instead; the operators of the
# box settled on one Postgres container per application, so it is back.
#
# THERE IS NO KEEL_DSN LINE ANY MORE AND THAT IS THE POINT. The DSN is composed
# inside docker-compose.prod.yml from the three variables below, so the password
# is written on this box exactly once and the hostname cannot be typed wrong. A
# KEEL_DSN set here is read by nothing.
#
# THE ROLE AND THE DATABASE MUST BOTH BE `keel`. scripts/migrate.sh's compose
# transport has `psql -U keel -d keel` written into it, so a different name here
# breaks the migration and nothing else, which is the hardest kind of break to
# find.
POSTGRES_DB=keel
POSTGRES_USER=keel
# Secret. `openssl rand -base64 32`. Postgres reads it ONLY when initdb runs on an
# empty volume: changing this line afterwards does not change the password in the
# database, it just stops every client from connecting. Section 9.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=REPLACE_WITH_A_GENERATED_PASSWORD

# The connection pool, and the deadline on the check `keel` makes before it
# serves anything. ALL FIVE ARE OPTIONAL and the values shown are the defaults
# in store.DefaultConfig, so leaving them commented changes nothing. A value
# that does not parse, or that is zero or negative, refuses to start rather than
# falling back, because a pool silently the wrong size is worse than a container
# that will not come up.
#
# The prefix is KEEL_, like everything else the binary reads. Nothing here reads
# a DATABASE_* variable: one set in .env is accepted by docker compose, ignored
# by the binary, and silent.
#
# 8 and 8 is 16 of the cluster's 100 connection slots, against a Postgres that
# serves nothing but Keel. There is no reason to lower them on this box.
#KEEL_DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=8
#KEEL_DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=4
#KEEL_DB_CONN_MAX_LIFETIME=30m
#KEEL_DB_CONN_MAX_IDLE_TIME=5m
# The database is one bridge away now, so this is unlikely to fire. It is what
# turns an unreachable database into an error in seconds instead of a hang.
#KEEL_DB_PING_TIMEOUT=5s

# NO KEEL_DOMAIN LINE, AND ADDING ONE BACK DOES NOTHING. It read "the hostname
# Caddy serves and gets a certificate for" until 11 September 2026, when Caddy was
# removed because the box already runs nginx. Caddy was its only consumer: no Go
# file reads it and no line of docker-compose.prod.yml does either. The hostname is
# a literal in scripts/deploy/nginx-keel.conf now. RUNBOOK.md section 2.

# WHERE THE API LISTENS ON THIS BOX, for nginx to proxy to. Optional: unset means
# 127.0.0.1:3000, which is what scripts/deploy/nginx-keel.conf proxies to. Set it
# only if another application here already holds 3000, checked with
# `ss -ltnp | grep 3000`, and change the proxy_pass port in that file to match.
# The two are one setting written in two places.
#
# THE 127.0.0.1 IS NOT DECORATION. Without it the API is served over plain HTTP on
# the public address, past nginx and past ufw, while `ufw status` looks correct.
# RUNBOOK.md section 3.2.
#KEEL_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1:3000

# The DASHBOARD's origins, not this API's own. Exact, comma separated, no
# wildcard. Add http://localhost:5173 and http://127.0.0.1:5173 for a dev
# server. Read once at startup, so changing it needs `up -d keel-serve`.
KEEL_CORS_ORIGINS=https://keels.app,https://www.keels.app

# REQUIRED SINCE 11 SEPTEMBER 2026, though it is written here as optional history.
# keel-serve's image reference lost its default in docker-compose.prod.yml, so
# compose now stops and names this variable when it is unset. keel-scan still
# defaults, so setting it once satisfies both.
KEEL_IMAGE=ghcr.io/keel-official/keel-backend

# Optional.
#KEEL_DUMP_KEEP_DAYS=14

# KEEL_ACME_EMAIL IS GONE. It bought Let's Encrypt expiry notices through Caddy's
# ACME client, and Caddy is no longer here. certbot takes the same thing as
# `--email` when it is first run, on the host. RUNBOOK.md section 3.8.

# NOT variables here, because compose does not run them. Both run on the HOST and
# reach the database through the loopback port docker-compose.prod.yml publishes,
# which is 5433 and not 5432: this box runs one Postgres per application and 5432
# belongs to the first one. Both need a client at least as new as the server,
# which is 18.4, and Ubuntu ships 16. See section 3.5.
#
#   KEEL_MIGRATE_DSN='postgres://keel:PASSWORD@localhost:5433/keel?sslmode=disable' bash scripts/migrate.sh
#   KEEL_DUMP_DSN='postgres://keel:PASSWORD@localhost:5433/keel?sslmode=disable' bash scripts/deploy/dump-database.sh
#
# The compose transport avoids the client-version problem entirely, because psql
# then runs inside the server's own container:
#
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.prod.yml bash scripts/migrate.sh
